<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="writing-assignment-2"
  xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Writing Assignment 2: Graphs That Tell a Story</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This assignment asks you to read a story from a graph, then build a graph from a story.
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Show your work; an answer with no supporting reasoning earns no credit.</p></li>
        <li><p>Every answer that asks you to describe or explain should be written in complete sentences.</p></li>
        <li><p>Upload a clean, high-quality scan of both sides to Blackboard when complete.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ===================================================
       PAGE 1: Alex's Hair (reading a story from a graph)
       =================================================== -->
  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-hair">
      <title>Alex's Hair</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          The graph below shows the length of Alex's hair, in inches, over <m>300</m> days.
          Day <m>0</m> is September 1. The dashed lines show moments when the length changed all
          at once.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-hair-graph">
          <caption>Length of Alex's hair over 300 days.</caption>
          <image xml:id="img-hair-graph" width="80%">
            <shortdescription>
              A graph of hair length that grows steadily, drops suddenly on day 60 and day 180,
              and grows steadily again after each drop.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                The horizontal axis is days since September 1, from 0 to 300 in steps of 30.
                The vertical axis is hair length in inches, from 0 to 5. The graph is a line
                segment from (0, 1) to (60, 2), then a dashed vertical drop from (60, 2) to
                (60, 0.5), then a line segment from (60, 0.5) to (180, 2.5), then a dashed
                vertical drop from (180, 2.5) to (180, 0), then a line segment from (180, 0)
                to (300, 2).
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="hair-graph">
              <diagram dimensions="(540,320)" margins="(60,50,20,18)">
                <coordinates bbox="(0,0,320,5.5)">
                  <grid-axes hticks="(0,30,300)" vticks="(0,1,5)" h-zero-label="yes">
                    <xlabel alignment="se">Days since September 1</xlabel>
                    <ylabel alignment="ne">Hair length (inches)</ylabel>
                  </grid-axes>

                  <!-- Growing pieces -->
                  <line endpoints="((0,1),(60,2))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="4"/>
                  <line endpoints="((60,0.5),(180,2.5))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="4"/>
                  <line endpoints="((180,0),(300,2))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="4"/>

                  <!-- Sudden changes -->
                  <line endpoints="((60,2),(60,0.5))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="4" dash="9 9"/>
                  <line endpoints="((180,2.5),(180,0))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="4" dash="9 9"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-hair-drops" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What happened to Alex's hair on day <m>60</m>? On day <m>180</m>? How can you tell
            from the graph?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-hair-length" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            On what days is Alex's hair exactly <m>1.5</m> inches long? Explain how you used the
            graph to find them.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-hair-rate" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Are the pieces of the graph where Alex's hair is growing all equally steep? What does
            that tell you about how Alex's hair grows? How many inches does it grow every
            <m>30</m> days?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-hair-predict" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose Alex doesn't get another haircut after day <m>300</m>. After how many total
            days will Alex's hair be <m>3</m> inches long for the first time? Explain how you
            found your answer.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-hair-story" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Write a short story, three or four sentences long, that describes everything the
            graph shows from day <m>0</m> to day <m>300</m>. Include the length of Alex's hair at
            the start, before and after each change, and at the end.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <!-- ===================================================
       PAGE 2: Maya's Checking Account (building a graph from a story)
       =================================================== -->
  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-maya">
      <title>Maya's Checking Account</title>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          On September 1, Maya has $400 in her checking account. Over the next 5 days she spends
          about the same amount each day on food and gas, and her balance falls to $200 by
          September 6. She doesn't spend anything from September 6 to September 8. On
          September 8, her paycheck of $700 is deposited. She doesn't spend anything else until
          September 10, when her $600 share of rent comes out of the account. From September 10
          to September 15 she spends steadily again, and she ends September 15 with $75.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task xml:id="ex-maya-table">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Fill in the table with the date and balance at each important moment.
          </p>
          <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
            <col width="34%" right="minor"/>
            <col width="22%" right="minor"/>
            <col width="22%"/>
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell>Moment</cell>
              <cell>Date</cell>
              <cell>Balance</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Start</cell><cell>Sept 1</cell><cell>$400</cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Stops spending</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Before paycheck</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>After paycheck</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>Before rent</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row bottom="minor"><cell>After rent</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
            <row><cell>End</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
          </tabular>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-maya-graph">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Plot the points from your table on the grid. Connect them in order with straight line
            segments. Choose a scale, number each axis evenly, and give each axis a title with
            units.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Hint: look back at how the haircuts show up on Alex's graph. The rent might look
            similar.</em>
          </p>
          <image xml:id="img-maya-grid" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>A blank grid with arrows on both axes and no labels.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A blank grid 15 squares wide and 9 squares tall, with an arrow along the bottom
                edge and an arrow along the left edge. There are no numbers or axis titles.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="maya-grid">
              <diagram dimensions="(440,290)" margins="(40,35,10,10)">
                <coordinates bbox="(0,0,15.8,9.8)">
                  <grid spacings="((0,1,15),(0,1,9))" stroke="lightgray"/>
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(15.6,0))" stroke="#3E4A59" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                  <line endpoints="((0,0),(0,9.6))" stroke="#3E4A59" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-maya-flat" workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What does the flat piece of your graph from September 6 to September 8 tell you about
            Maya's account?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-maya-rate" workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Did Maya spend money faster from September 1 to 6, or from September 10 to 15? Find
            how much she spent per day in each stretch, and explain how you can see the
            difference on your graph.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>

      <task xml:id="ex-maya-shape" workspace="1.25in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The paycheck and the rent change the balance all at once, but the daily spending
            changes it a little at a time. Explain how this difference shows up in the shape of
            your graph.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <!-- ===================================================
       INSTRUCTOR KEY (not rendered)

       Alex's Hair
       Drops: Day 60, haircut from 2 in to 0.5 in. Day 180, head shaved from
         2.5 in to 0. The graph drops straight down at those days.
       Length 1.5 in: days 30, 120, 270 (horizontal line at 1.5).
         Hair grows 0.5 in every 30 days: 1 in at day 0 gives 1.5 at day 30;
         0.5 in at day 60 gives 1.5 at day 120; 0 at day 180 gives 1.5 at day 270.
       Rate: all growing pieces are equally steep; steady growth of 0.5 in
         every 30 days.
       Predict: day 360. At day 300 it is 2 in; 1 more inch takes 60 days.
         It never reached 3 in earlier (max before day 300 was 2.5 in).
       Story: answers vary. Starts at 1 in, grows to 2 in by day 60, cut to
         0.5 in, grows to 2.5 in by day 180, shaved to 0, grows to 2 in by day 300.

       Maya's Checking Account
       Table: Start Sept 1 $400; Stops spending Sept 6 $200; Before paycheck
         Sept 8 $200; After paycheck Sept 8 $900; Before rent Sept 10 $900;
         After rent Sept 10 $300; End Sept 15 $75.
       Graph: 1 day per box horizontally, $100 per box vertically works.
         Falls (1,400) to (6,200), flat to (8,200), straight up to (8,900),
         flat to (10,900), straight down to (10,300), falls to (15,75).
         Accept a slanted rent line from Sept 9 to Sept 10 if the rest matches.
       Flat piece: balance not changing; no spending, no deposits.
       Rates: Sept 1 to 6 is $200 over 5 days, $40 per day. Sept 10 to 15 is
         $225 over 5 days, $45 per day. Faster in the second stretch; that
         piece is steeper.
       Shape: single-day events are vertical jumps (like the haircuts);
         gradual spending is a slanted line.
       =================================================== -->

</worksheet>